  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Subtropical Storm Josephine located well off the Mid-\nAtlantic coast will continue to move off to the northeast through \nthe end of the week, with coastal impacts and hazardous marine \nconditions gradually dwindling.\n\nSubtropical Storm Josephine located well off the Mid-Atlantic coast \nwill continue to move off to the northeast through the end of the \nweek. Impacts will continue to dwindle for our coastal and marine \nareas. The multi-day north-northeast wind has resulted in elevated \nseas that will continue to lessen through the end of the week and \ninto the weekend. These big waves breaking onto the beaches will \nresult in some additional beach erosion, especially during the times \nof high tide. This will continue to lessen each day through the \nweekend.\n\nAn incoming system from the northwest will push across the region \ntonight, but no precipitation and minimal cloud cover is expected \nwith it. Its main function is to move Josephine away. This is what \nwill help further reduce the marine and coastal impacts, while \nproviding a fresh shot of chilly air. A lingering tight pressure \ngradient into tonight starts to lessen tomorrow morning as high \npressure builds into the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Subtropical \nStorm Josephine well of the coast resulting in widespread minor \ncoastal flooding continuing in Ocean County, NJ and Sussex County, \nDE.\n\nAs Subtropical Storm Josephine lingers well off the coast elevated \ntide cycles are persisting some areas. \n\nIn Ocean County, New Jersey and Sussex County, Delaware, water \nlevels remain elevated and minor tidal flooding is continuing, \nparticularly in the back bays. In fact a few locations in the back \nbays came very close to or even touched moderate levels of coastal \nflooding earlier today with the high tide. It's going to take some \ntime for water levels to diminish in these areas so we've extended \nthe Coastal Flood Advisory for these counties through Friday \nevening. Elsewhere along the Atlantic coast and Delaware Bay any \nadditional coastal flooding looks to be spotty at most. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for the \nMaryland eastern shore. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Frost and freeze impacts possible Friday night and \nSaturday night where the growing season continues. \n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast tomorrow, a \npotentially strong mid-level ridge in the Plains to Midwest \nshifts eastward some into the weekend. This will support strong \nsurface high pressure initially centered near the Great Lakes \nand portions of eastern Canada, which then shifts eastward into \nthe Northeast and Mid-Atlantic tomorrow and over the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface high, \nand as the high itself settles more overhead, the winds will \ndecouple. This will set the stage for efficient radiational cooling \nboth Friday night and Saturday night. The forecast lows are in the \n30s for much of the area (away from the coasts and outside of the \nurban centers) with a frost and freeze potential. Due to the \ncontinued forecast trends, a Freeze Watch was issued for locations \nNW of the I-95 corridor where the growing season remains active. \nThis is for Friday night as temperatures in these areas are at or \nnear freezing. Frost Advisories may be needed outside of these \nareas for Friday night, especially if the radiational cooling \nends up being stronger.",
